1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,718
Greetings, fellow aliens!

2
00:00:02,126 --> 00:00:04,380
This is the tenth episode of Earthlings 101.

3
00:00:04,888 --> 00:00:08,887
Today we will learn about the earthlings favourite form of communication: language.

4
00:00:42,192 --> 00:00:46,872
A language is an acoustical code, produced by the mouth, and perceived by the ears.

5
00:00:47,392 --> 00:00:52,499
Information is encoded in sound groups called words which are strung together to sequences called sentences.

6
00:00:52,942 --> 00:00:59,082
A sentence may, for example, transmit the information that a specific vehicle is infested by a certain kind of animals.

7
00:00:59,082 --> 00:01:02,162
Language can also be transmitted in visual form.

8
00:01:02,502 --> 00:01:03,571
This is called writing.

9
00:01:04,015 --> 00:01:06,095
We will speak about writing in another episode.

10
00:01:06,815 --> 00:01:09,915
The many languages on earth have their roots a long time ago.

11
00:01:10,295 --> 00:01:16,607
As earthling history is a turmoil of conquest and migration, languages frequently blend, fusionate and split.

12
00:01:17,210 --> 00:01:19,535
Also, languages are constantly developing.

13
00:01:19,959 --> 00:01:21,579
We will come back to this point later.

14
00:01:22,659 --> 00:01:27,560
In order to understand language, we have to understand how earthlings modelize the world in their mind.

15
00:01:27,560 --> 00:01:33,962
Most aliens see the physical world as what is commonly known as the "conglomerate" - some kind of four-dimensional energy soup in spacetime.

16
00:01:35,247 --> 00:01:41,787
But earthlings don't see reality this way: First, for earthlings, space and time are two wholly separate things.

17
00:01:42,427 --> 00:01:46,485
Secondly, their view of the world is all about matter: Energy at sub-light speed.

18
00:01:47,015 --> 00:01:52,275
To describe matter, they have three concepts: Things, properties, and actions.

19
00:01:53,595 --> 00:01:56,530
Things are, so to say, the noodles in the energy soup.

20
00:01:56,795 --> 00:02:00,099
A thing is a connected bunch of matter, usually with a distinctive shape.

21
00:02:00,568 --> 00:02:02,488
For example, a fruit is a thing.

22
00:02:02,988 --> 00:02:06,120
Half a fruit is not a thing, unless you cut the fruit in two.

23
00:02:06,590 --> 00:02:09,585
Two fruits aren't one thing, unless they are somehow connected.

24
00:02:10,098 --> 00:02:14,299
Then again, a fruit tree is also a thing, as is a fruit seed.

25
00:02:14,299 --> 00:02:21,478
In a larger sense, a thing can be anything that has a shape and looks like a connected bunch of matter, like the moon, a cloud or a shadow.

26
00:02:21,880 --> 00:02:28,020
According to Sixty Symbols, the "biggest thing in the universe" is a 4 billion light years long cluster of 73 quasars.

27
00:02:29,160 --> 00:02:32,221
Things are described by thing-words, or nouns.

28
00:02:32,221 --> 00:02:41,525
Now, you can't create a new word for every thing in the world, so earthlings group similar things into a single category and use the same word - for example, "star".

29
00:02:41,945 --> 00:02:50,625
All multicellular creatures capable of photosynthesis are "plants", all big plants with wooden stems are "trees", and all mobile creatures that feed on other creatures are "animals".

30
00:02:52,005 --> 00:02:54,025
Except for the microbes.

31
00:02:57,565 --> 00:03:04,658
Using the same word for similar things is not only a trick to simplify language: Grouping things into categories is a basic feature of the earthling mind.

32
00:03:05,101 --> 00:03:12,541
It helps to gain and retain knowledge - for example, the knowledge that you better run if you see anything that fits into the category "zombie velociraptor".

33
00:03:12,881 --> 00:03:16,952
A second concept is properties : Labels attached to things by the mind.

34
00:03:17,316 --> 00:03:21,776
A property is a common attribute shared by several things, like "big", or "green".

35
00:03:22,276 --> 00:03:25,675
Properties are, so to say, the taste of the noodles in the soup.

36
00:03:25,897 --> 00:03:29,239
They are specified by another kind of words, adjectives.

37
00:03:30,100 --> 00:03:38,155
Now, things and properties are great to describe the status quo, a slice of spacetime - but to describe temporal processes, we need something more.

38
00:03:38,517 --> 00:03:41,257
That's where the third concept comes in: Actions.

39
00:03:41,777 --> 00:03:46,499
An action is any process that changes either the position of one or more objects, or their properties.

40
00:03:47,779 --> 00:03:52,219
An action is generally thought to originate from one thing, the "subject" of the action.

41
00:03:52,679 --> 00:03:55,952
Some actions influence other things, the "objects" of the action.

42
00:03:56,296 --> 00:04:01,176
This concept is closely linked to causality - a concept I will explain in another episode.

43
00:04:02,056 --> 00:04:04,876
Consider, for example, a fruit falling off a tree.

44
00:04:05,296 --> 00:04:11,024
For most aliens, this is simply a four-dimensional energy cluster, aligned to a geodesic in the conglomerate.

45
00:04:11,024 --> 00:04:16,504
Not so for earthlings: They see this as an "action" which changes the location of the fruit in space.

46
00:04:16,884 --> 00:04:21,304
Most earthlings would say that the fruit is the "subject" of the action: The fruit falls.

47
00:04:21,544 --> 00:04:28,284
An earthling physicist might say that the earth attracts the fruit - thus, the earth is the subject, the fruit is the object.

48
00:04:28,724 --> 00:04:31,344
A gardener might say that the tree drops its fruits.

49
00:04:31,549 --> 00:04:36,537
A religious earthling might believe that it was the will of god, and a conspiracy theorist may blame the Freemasons.

50
00:04:37,422 --> 00:04:42,642
No matter how they turn it, there is always an action and a subject, and sometimes an object.

51
00:04:43,282 --> 00:04:47,439
Actions are described by action-words, or verbs, like "fall" or "eat".

52
00:04:47,803 --> 00:04:52,028
Verbs are usually grouped with a word for the subject, for example "Earthling eats".

53
00:04:52,528 --> 00:04:59,678
Additional information is added in form of other words, for example for objects and properties, like "The earthling eats the red apple near the tree".

54
00:04:59,960 --> 00:05:01,200
This is called a sentence.

55
00:05:01,840 --> 00:05:07,942
Note that the order of words is important - the sentence "The red tree eats the earthling near the apple" means something completely different.

56
00:05:08,204 --> 00:05:18,964
Tips for tourists: When you acquire an universal translator with English, Mandarin, Spanish, Russian, French and Arabic, you can get along in most regions of the world.

57
00:05:19,404 --> 00:05:23,344
However, make sure it has an insult filter, otherwise you may get into trouble.

58
00:05:24,184 --> 00:05:30,844
But how do earthlings think and speak about more non-material matters like space, time, relationships, mathematics, etc.?

59
00:05:31,284 --> 00:05:33,209
That's where abstraction comes in.

60
00:05:33,409 --> 00:05:44,449
For earthlings, there are several levels of abstraction: The body, the physical world, space, time, more abstract stuff like social relationships, and pure abstract concepts.

61
00:05:44,949 --> 00:05:48,622
Earthlings think about the abstract just like they think about the physical world.

62
00:05:48,622 --> 00:05:51,622
Every clearly delimited entity is, for example, a thing.

63
00:05:52,062 --> 00:05:55,875
They even reuse concrete words to speak about abstract ideas.

64
00:05:56,477 --> 00:05:59,552
Using words for one thing to describe a different thing is called a metaphor.

65
00:06:00,356 --> 00:06:04,560
Numbers, for example, are usually described with vertical spatial metaphors.

66
00:06:04,721 --> 00:06:07,724
Figures are high, low, they rise, fall.

67
00:06:08,248 --> 00:06:11,348
This comes from the earthling habit of stacking merchandises vertically.

68
00:06:11,948 --> 00:06:14,853
Time is rather described with horizontal spatial metaphors.

69
00:06:15,037 --> 00:06:17,773
Before, after, back in time, time runs.

70
00:06:17,773 --> 00:06:20,053
This comes from horizontal movement.

71
00:06:20,413 --> 00:06:27,920
Common galactic languages are usually developed by supercomputers, creatures of pure logic but who had to learn about time, space and reality.

72
00:06:28,384 --> 00:06:32,424
In consequence, those languages have been developed from the abstract to the concrete.

73
00:06:32,964 --> 00:06:37,524
Earthling languages, however, develop the other way round: From concrete to abstract.

74
00:06:38,064 --> 00:06:42,844
Many spatial and temporal expressions come even from the lowest level of abstraction: The body.

75
00:06:42,844 --> 00:06:48,354
An earthling's "back", the "back" of a house, "moving backwards", "back in the 19th century" etc.

76
00:06:48,987 --> 00:06:52,255
Interestingly, some spatial concepts come from animal bodies.

77
00:06:52,520 --> 00:06:56,806
The "head" of an animal, the "head" of a train, "looking ahead", "being ahead of one's time"...

78
00:06:57,289 --> 00:06:59,698
Earthlings call this the "zoomorphic model".

79
00:06:59,698 --> 00:07:03,378
But metaphors are also useful to create more expressive formulations.

80
00:07:03,898 --> 00:07:18,008
Take earthling mating rituals, for example: Instead of saying "I like you, wanna have sex?", one might say "You are the love of my life, my sun and moon and stars, the key to my heart, the sodium to my chlorine and the parmesan to my spaghetti, wanna have sex"?

81
00:07:18,931 --> 00:07:24,451
Metaphors can't be underrated - actually, we will see that they are one of the key mechanisms in how language has formed.

82
00:07:25,091 --> 00:07:26,731
Language is in constant development.

83
00:07:27,411 --> 00:07:30,387
Interestingly, earthlings perceive this as a constant decline.

84
00:07:30,917 --> 00:07:33,358
Take, for example, the language called English.

85
00:07:33,846 --> 00:07:39,543
According to many modern earthlings, the English language is in a sorry state and was much more perfect a century earlier.

86
00:07:39,543 --> 00:07:58,141
But if you travel into the past and ask writers and savants of past eras, you will hear similar complaints - be it from George Orwell in 1946, Harry Thurston Peck in 1899, August Schleicher in 1848, Thomas Sheridan in 1780, Jonathan Swift in 1712, or Thomas Sprat in 1667.

87
00:07:58,442 --> 00:08:00,221
You'll find the full quotes in the doobly-do.

88
00:08:00,717 --> 00:08:09,830
If there is any consensus amongst linguists of past centuries, it's probably that the most perfect language of all time was classical Latin, two thousand solar cycles ago.

89
00:08:10,290 --> 00:08:13,293
Latin scholars of that time, however, would have begged to differ.

90
00:08:13,656 --> 00:08:19,734
In 46 B.C., for instance, Cicero complained that the Latin of his time was far less perfect than a century earlier.

91
00:08:19,916 --> 00:08:24,048
What earthlings perceive as decline is actually a perpetual , cyclic, evolution.

92
00:08:24,536 --> 00:08:28,263
It's like the perceived downfall of a spacecraft which is actually an orbit.

93
00:08:28,263 --> 00:08:31,775
This perpetual evolution of language can be compared to the Heteromorphs of Galarius.

94
00:08:32,602 --> 00:08:39,839
Those polymorphic creatures live in the high atmospheric pressure of the gas giant Galarius Seven and are coordinated by a global telepathic field.

95
00:08:40,222 --> 00:08:46,733
One could compare single creatures to words, body parts to syllables, and groups of creatures to expressions and sentences.

96
00:08:47,095 --> 00:08:53,493
The constant change of the Heteromorphs is based on four mechanisms: Fusion, Erosion, Assimilation and Combination.

97
00:08:53,493 --> 00:09:01,613
Fusion means that due to the high atmospheric pressure, groups of creatures are fused, so several individuals become mere organs of one single organism.

98
00:09:02,133 --> 00:09:05,847
Erosion means that body parts are constantly eroded by violent storms.

99
00:09:06,513 --> 00:09:14,118
Assimilation means that the telepathic field of the population makes creatures of the same kind more and more similar, such that they get identical members and organs.

100
00:09:14,118 --> 00:09:18,153
But through the process of erosion and assimilation, creatures become weaker.

101
00:09:18,395 --> 00:09:24,135
Hence the fourth mechanism: Combination, the capacity to form groups which are able to do wholly new tasks.

102
00:09:25,215 --> 00:09:34,140
It's easy to see that this forms an endless loop: Creatures form groups, fuse together, are assimilated and eroded, become smaller and weaker, combine again etc.

103
00:09:35,040 --> 00:09:40,960
Now, Earthling languages follow a similar cycle: Fusion, Erosion, Assimilation, and Metaphors.

104
00:09:41,740 --> 00:09:47,132
Fusion means that groups of words tend to fuse together, often forming suffixes and prefixes.

105
00:09:47,673 --> 00:09:51,102
This creates what earthlings call conjugations and declinations.

106
00:09:51,546 --> 00:09:55,718
Erosion means that words are shortened and sounds shift such that they are easier to pronounce.

107
00:09:56,601 --> 00:10:00,058
Assimilation means that differences between words tend to be smoothed.

108
00:10:00,058 --> 00:10:03,950
For example, conjugations of verbs become more and more similar.

109
00:10:04,292 --> 00:10:06,475
This is how general grammar rules form.

110
00:10:06,843 --> 00:10:13,149
Those three mechanisms come from earthlings' laziness: They make language simpler to pronounce and rules easier to memorize.

111
00:10:13,532 --> 00:10:16,332
But they also reduce the expressiveness of words.

112
00:10:16,652 --> 00:10:19,844
That's where the fourth mechanism comes into play: Metaphors.

113
00:10:20,046 --> 00:10:26,846
As mentioned before, metaphors mean that words, or groups of words, are used to express something completely new.

114
00:10:27,346 --> 00:10:29,646
This is the main constructive mechanism of language.

115
00:10:30,826 --> 00:10:42,492
Like the life cycle of heteromorphs, the evolution of language is an endless cycle of creation and destruction: Words are combined to new, colorful metaphors, they fuse, are eroded and assimilated until they are mutilated and worn-out.

116
00:10:43,515 --> 00:10:47,029
Eventually they are combined to new expressive metaphors, and so on.

117
00:10:47,213 --> 00:10:51,718
The whole building of language is nothing else than an asteroid reef of dead metaphors.

118
00:10:51,718 --> 00:11:02,238
In other words, the etymology of language is inherently fractal: Words are old groups of words, which were old groups of words, which were old groups of words, and so on.

119
00:11:04,378 --> 00:11:05,418
Strategic advice.

120
00:11:06,478 --> 00:11:08,138
Earthlings' language can be misleading.

121
00:11:08,462 --> 00:11:17,732
For example, the frequent use of the word "tanks" in radio messages may lead you to the conclusion that the earthlings will use large tank vehicles to spray your troops with deadly hydric acid.

122
00:11:18,275 --> 00:11:25,997
However, once you have equipped all your soldiers with protective gear against hydric acid, you may discover that military tanks are actually something completely different.

123
00:11:26,957 --> 00:11:30,410
To demonstrate the evolution of language, let's look at the english word "gonna".

124
00:11:31,173 --> 00:11:34,785
In modern English, this word indicates near future: "We're gonna eat".

125
00:11:35,091 --> 00:11:39,219
It's an abbreviation of "going to", but only in the sense "about to", not "walking to".

126
00:11:39,826 --> 00:11:45,142
Hundreds of solar cycles ago, "going to" was only used in the context "going or traveling to a place".

127
00:11:45,625 --> 00:11:56,092
Then, in what earthlings call the 15th century, "going to do something" appeared, but only in the sense "going somewhere, in order to do something", for example soldiers who are going to bring a prisoner to a castle.

128
00:11:56,313 --> 00:12:06,347
From there, the meaning shifted gradually towards a more abstract meaning, and in the seventeenth century, we read of an arms depot which is "going to be taken away" - the arms depot is certainly not walking anywhere.

129
00:12:06,829 --> 00:12:12,392
So, "going to" has become, as a grammar manual from 1646 puts it, "a sign of the future".

130
00:12:12,837 --> 00:12:15,584
From there, the future "going to" became more and more popular.

131
00:12:16,199 --> 00:12:23,519
Then, at the beginning of the twentieth century, an eroded variant of "going to" appears: The word "gonna", as it is used till this day.

132
00:12:26,759 --> 00:12:36,919
Scientific advice: Many earthlings believe that language and logic are uniquely processed by the left half of the brain, whereas things like creativity, images and music are solely processed by the right half.

133
00:12:37,139 --> 00:12:38,379
That is utter nonsense.

134
00:12:38,979 --> 00:12:50,019
So, if you swap the left halves of the brains of a french-speaking painter and an english-speaking musician, you don't get a french-speaking musician and an english-speaking painter - you just get two very messed up earthlings.

135
00:12:51,319 --> 00:12:54,619
But now, let's travel into the future of the English language.

136
00:12:55,039 --> 00:12:56,454
Consider the verb "To have".

137
00:12:56,975 --> 00:13:02,539
Traditionally, this is conjugated "I have, you have, he has, we have, you have, they have".

138
00:13:02,779 --> 00:13:09,979
In the present, erosion has created the shorter forms: I've, You've, He's, We've, You've, They've.

139
00:13:10,139 --> 00:13:13,712
The pronoun and the verb are about to fuse into one single word.

140
00:13:13,915 --> 00:13:19,566
In some regions, the plural "You've", has been replaced by "Y'all've", a short form of "You all have".

141
00:13:20,189 --> 00:13:28,734
In a hundred solar cycles, the apostrophe will be dropped and the pronouns will have become prefixes: Ive, Youve, Hes, Weve, Yallve, Theyve.

142
00:13:28,915 --> 00:13:36,415
Some centuries later, the conjugation will be simplified through assimilation to Ive, Youve, Heve, Weve, Yallve, Theyve.

143
00:13:36,835 --> 00:13:41,800
Eventually, erosion will replace "Ive", by the easier to pronounce "Eve", and "Yallve", by "Yave".

144
00:13:42,120 --> 00:13:49,960
Eight hundred solar cycles from now, all english verbs will have adapted the same prefixes for present tense - again, through assimilation: Eetalk, Youtalk, Hetalk, Wetalk, Yatalk, Theetalk, and the future tense will be marked by an eroded "gonna": Eenatalk, Younatalk, Henatalk, Wenatalk, Yanatalk, Theenatalk, and the past tense a crippled, "have": Eevetalk, Youvetalk, Hevetalk, Wevetalk, Yavetalk, Theevetalk.

145
00:14:11,160 --> 00:14:15,434
But in the same time, the mechanics of metaphor will build new colorful expressions.

146
00:14:16,038 --> 00:14:22,040
For example, a thousand years from now, the invention of time travel will require a whole new set of grammatical tenses.

147
00:14:22,364 --> 00:14:29,364
For instance, to say that someone did a time jump to escape death, one would say "Heve jum fro’die", "He has jumped from dying".

148
00:14:29,964 --> 00:14:35,408
So, the verb "jump", originally a body movement, becomes an auxiliary verb for time-travel tenses.

149
00:14:36,488 --> 00:14:41,124
By the way, this episode is partially based upon the earthling book "The Unfolding of Language".

150
00:14:41,466 --> 00:14:44,346
It describes in great detail how language has developed.

151
00:14:44,826 --> 00:14:45,863
I put a link into the Doobly-doo.

152
00:14:46,757 --> 00:14:52,673
A personal remark: When you want to be a Youtube partner or make money with your videos, you need an AdSense account.

153
00:14:53,135 --> 00:14:56,163
Like most Youtubers, I took care of respecting the rules.

154
00:14:56,753 --> 00:15:02,526
Nevertheless, AdSense has closed my account for "invalid activity", without telling me what I am actually accused of.

155
00:15:02,968 --> 00:15:08,207
This means three things: I will continue making videos, but I will never be able to make money with them.

156
00:15:08,669 --> 00:15:11,876
Secondly, I can't never again become a partner or join a network.

157
00:15:12,302 --> 00:15:17,293
And third, I have no idea for what I'm punished - I can only guess that someone click-bombed me.

158
00:15:17,557 --> 00:15:20,077
I will probably make a video about this incident.

159
00:15:21,977 --> 00:15:24,542
This was the tenth episode of Earthlings 101.

160
00:15:25,055 --> 00:15:30,677
Next time we'll talk about something that is crucial to understanding all kinds of group behaviour: Rituals.

161
00:15:31,121 --> 00:15:35,025
This will also explain why earthlings like t-shirts with strange symbols.

162
00:15:35,412 --> 00:15:39,101
Thanks for watching, and as always: Don't forget to be alien!

